The fix belongs in `reef/storage/sql_data.py`. `SQLDataStore.update` (lines 515-535) reads each item's metadata, applies the merge patch in Python, and writes it back. It takes no lock. `create` and `delete` both call `self.lock_creates(connection)` first, but `update` does not. So two concurrent patches to one item can each read the same old `metadata_json`, and the second write drops the first one's `scenarios` uses. The likely fix is to call `self.lock_creates(connection)` at the start of `update`, before `self.rows(...)`.

`reef/storage/data.py` documents the `update` contract (`DataStore.update`, around line 405). It may need a docstring note, but it is not where the bug is. I did not open the Postgres override or the tests, and I did not make any edits.

