The change belongs mainly in `reef/dispatcher/serving.py`. The serving worker keeps a per-scenario `activated` map (line 98), and its stop path (`run_item`, around lines 205-220) calls `runtime.stop_serving(served)` without clearing that entry. The next activation of the same artifact then sees `self.activated[item.scenario] == artifact_id` at line 222 and skips the activation. The fix is to drop that scenario's entry when the worker stops the endpoint that serves the artifact.

The other candidates are supporting files. `reef/inference/serving.py` holds the `_adopted` and `loaded_artifact_ids` state used on redeploy and restart, which the tests for "no engine for a scenario that serves nothing" would exercise. `reef/dispatcher/served.py` builds the `desired` state that decides which scenarios are served. I did not open the test files, and I did not confirm the exact `ServedArtifact` field that names the scenario, so the fix may need a small adjustment there.

